window.QUIZ.push(

// ---- Streaming ----

{id:"q14-001", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A retailer publishes point-of-sale events to an Amazon Kinesis data stream. Two custom applications already read the stream to update stock levels and to detect fraud, and they must keep working unchanged. The analytics team now wants the same events loaded into a table in the company's Amazon Redshift data warehouse within a few minutes of arrival. The team does not want to write or operate another consumer application. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Build a third consumer with the Kinesis Client Library on EC2 instances that batches records and inserts them into Redshift",
    "Create an Amazon Data Firehose stream with the Kinesis data stream as its source and Amazon Redshift as its destination",
    "Replace the Kinesis data stream with an Amazon Data Firehose stream and point the producers and both applications at it",
    "Increase the stream's retention period and schedule a nightly AWS Glue job that copies the retained records into Redshift"],
 a:[1],
 x:["This works, but it is exactly the consumer application the team does not want to write, scale and patch.",
    "Firehose has a prebuilt integration with Kinesis Data Streams: it reads the existing stream next to the other consumers, stages the data in S3 and issues the Redshift COPY command, with no application to write.",
    "Firehose delivers records to the one destination configured on it; it is not the stream that the stock and fraud applications read today, so both would have to be rebuilt.",
    "A nightly job loads the events many hours after they arrive; the requirement is within a few minutes."],
 ref:["d6c3"], doc:"firehose"},

{id:"q14-002", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A lending company scores loan applications from events in an Amazon Kinesis data stream that runs in provisioned mode with the default retention period. Data scientists release a new scoring model every quarter. Before each release they must run the model against the previous 90 days of events, in their original order, with the same consumer code that reads the stream in production. Which change meets this requirement with the LEAST development effort?",
 o:["Increase the stream's data retention period to at least 90 days",
    "Register the scoring application as an enhanced fan-out consumer",
    "Switch the stream's capacity mode from provisioned to on-demand",
    "Archive the stream to Amazon S3 and write a tool that re-sends the objects"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A stream keeps records for 24 hours by default and for up to 365 days when the retention period is raised, and consumers can read the retained records again in the same order.",
    "Enhanced fan-out gives a consumer its own read throughput per shard; it does not keep records any longer than the retention period.",
    "On-demand mode manages shard capacity for throughput; records still leave the stream when the retention period ends.",
    "An archive in S3 keeps the events, but replaying them needs a new tool and a second code path, which is more development than changing one stream setting."],
 ref:[], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q14-003", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A food delivery platform writes order status events (placed, accepted, picked up, delivered) to an Amazon Kinesis data stream with 12 shards. To spread the load, producers set the partition key of every record to a random UUID. A consumer that builds each order's timeline sometimes processes 'delivered' before 'picked up' for the same order. Each order's events must be processed in the sequence in which they were written, and the stream's capacity must not be reduced. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Merge the shards step by step until the stream consists of a single shard",
    "Register the timeline consumer for enhanced fan-out on the stream",
    "Have the producers use the order ID as the partition key of every record",
    "Increase the data retention period of the stream to seven days"],
 a:[2],
 x:["One shard would hold all records in one sequence, but it would also cut the stream to a twelfth of its write capacity, which the requirement rules out.",
    "Enhanced fan-out gives the consumer dedicated read throughput and faster delivery; it does not change which shard a record is written to.",
    "The partition key decides which shard a record goes to, and a shard is one ordered sequence of records. With the order ID as the key, all events of an order land in the same shard and are read in the order written, while different orders still spread over 12 shards.",
    "Retention controls how long records stay readable, not the order in which records of one order are read."],
 ref:[], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q14-004", day:14, dom:1, tags:["Streaming","KMS & encryption"],
 q:"A healthcare company sends patient monitoring data to an Amazon Kinesis data stream from hundreds of producer applications that it cannot change quickly. A new policy requires the records to be encrypted at rest inside the stream with a key that the company's security team creates and whose key policy the team controls. Consumers run on Amazon EC2 with an instance role. Which combination of actions meets these requirements with the LEAST change to the applications? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable server-side encryption on the stream and select a customer managed AWS KMS key",
    "Enable server-side encryption on the stream with the AWS managed key <code>aws/kinesis</code>",
    "Add client-side encryption with an encryption library to every producer and consumer",
    "Allow the producers' roles <code>kms:GenerateDataKey</code> and the consumers' role <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the key",
    "Require all producers and consumers to call the Kinesis endpoints over HTTPS only"],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Server-side encryption encrypts records before they are written to the stream's storage and decrypts them when they are read, so producers and consumers need no cryptographic code, and a customer managed key is one the security team creates and controls.",
    "<code>aws/kinesis</code> is the key that the Kinesis service generates for the account; the policy asks for a key that the security team creates and controls.",
    "Client-side encryption would satisfy the policy but means changing and redeploying every producer and consumer.",
    "With a customer managed key, producers must be allowed <code>kms:GenerateDataKey</code> and consumers <code>kms:Decrypt</code>; without these permissions writes and reads on the stream fail.",
    "HTTPS protects the data in transit to and from the service; the policy is about encryption at rest inside the stream."],
 ref:[], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q14-005", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A media company is building a new event platform. Its developers have standardized on the Apache Kafka client libraries, and a third-party analytics tool can consume only from Kafka topics. Traffic is hard to predict: almost idle overnight and very high during live broadcasts. The small platform team does not want to choose broker sizes, add brokers, or rebalance partitions across brokers as traffic changes. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["An Amazon MSK Provisioned cluster with Standard brokers sized for the broadcast peak",
    "An Amazon Kinesis data stream in on-demand capacity mode with enhanced fan-out",
    "Apache Kafka on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group across three Availability Zones",
    "An Amazon MSK Serverless cluster that the Kafka clients and the tool connect to"],
 a:[3],
 x:["A provisioned cluster is scaled in units of brokers that the team has to choose and change, and sizing for the peak leaves the cluster oversized overnight.",
    "A stream in on-demand mode scales by itself, but applications reach it through the Kinesis API and client libraries, not as Kafka topics, so the Kafka clients and the Kafka-only tool do not fit.",
    "Self-managed Kafka leaves installation, patching, scaling and broker replacement with the team.",
    "MSK Serverless is fully compatible with Apache Kafka and provisions and scales capacity automatically while it manages the partitions of the topics, so there are no brokers to size."],
 ref:[], doc:"msk"},

{id:"q14-006", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A logistics company runs Apache Flink applications, written in Java with the DataStream API, on a self-managed cluster of EC2 instances. The applications read from Amazon MSK topics, keep state to join and aggregate events over time windows, and write the results to another topic. Engineers spend much of their time patching the cluster, resizing it, and restoring application state after failures. The company wants to keep the application code and remove that work. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Rewrite the logic as AWS Lambda functions that are triggered by the MSK topics",
    "Run the applications on Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink",
    "Move the applications to Apache Flink on a long-running Amazon EMR cluster",
    "Send the topics to Amazon Data Firehose with a Lambda transformation function"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Lambda can consume MSK topics, but the windowed joins and their state would have to be rebuilt in new code, and the requirement is to keep the Flink applications.",
    "The service runs open-source Apache Flink applications and takes over provisioning compute, resilience to an Availability Zone failure, automatic scaling, and application backups as checkpoints and snapshots; the Flink APIs are used as on a self-hosted cluster.",
    "Amazon EMR can run Flink, but a long-running EMR cluster on EC2 is still a cluster to configure, resize and look after.",
    "Firehose delivers records to a destination and can transform each batch with a function; the stateful joins over time windows would have to be rewritten, and the Flink code would be dropped."],
 ref:[], doc:"flink"},

{id:"q14-007", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A company sells video doorbells. When a visitor presses the button, the homeowner must see live video and talk with the visitor from a mobile app, with latency low enough for a natural conversation. The company does not want to build or scale signaling and media relay servers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon Kinesis Video Streams with HLS playback of the doorbell stream in the app",
    "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the doorbell and the app as producers and consumers",
    "Amazon Kinesis Video Streams with WebRTC between the doorbell and the app",
    "Amazon Data Firehose delivering the doorbell video to S3 for the app to download"],
 a:[2],
 x:["HLS playback is one-way viewing, and its latency for live playback is typically between 3 and 5 seconds, too slow for a conversation.",
    "Kinesis Data Streams collects data records for processing applications; live two-way media between a device and a player is what Kinesis Video Streams provides.",
    "Kinesis Video Streams with WebRTC is a fully managed capability for low-latency live streaming and two-way audio or video between camera devices and mobile or web players, with no signaling or media relay servers to operate.",
    "Firehose buffers records and delivers them to a store such as S3; that gives recordings to download, not a live two-way call."],
 ref:[], doc:"kvs"},

// ---- Analytics ----

{id:"q14-008", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A retailer's partners drop new CSV files under an S3 prefix throughout the day. A scheduled AWS Glue ETL job runs every hour, converts the files to Parquet, and appends them to a curated table. The job currently reads every file under the prefix on each run, so run times keep growing and the curated table contains duplicate rows. The team wants each run to process only the files that arrived since the last successful run, with the least development effort. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Enable job bookmarks for the AWS Glue job",
    "Run an AWS Glue crawler before each job run",
    "Allocate more workers to the AWS Glue job",
    "Expire the source files with an S3 Lifecycle rule"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A job bookmark is state that AWS Glue keeps from earlier runs of a job; with it, a scheduled job processes only new data, which prevents reprocessing and duplicate data in the target.",
    "A crawler updates table definitions and partitions in the Data Catalog; it does not record which files a job has already processed.",
    "More workers shorten a run, but every run still reads all files and still appends the old rows again.",
    "Lifecycle expiration works in whole days, so files are still read by many hourly runs before they are removed, and a file could be deleted before a failed run has processed it."],
 ref:[], doc:"glue"},

{id:"q14-009", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A data engineering team launches a transient Amazon EMR cluster each night to run Apache Hive and Apache Spark jobs on data in Amazon S3, and terminates the cluster when the jobs finish. The table definitions are lost with every cluster because the Hive metastore is on the primary node, so the first job of each night recreates them. Analysts also want to query the same tables during the day with Amazon Athena. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Keep the cluster running all day so that the metastore on its primary node stays available",
    "Create an external Hive metastore in an Amazon RDS for MySQL database for the clusters",
    "Export the table definitions to S3 after each run and re-import them with a bootstrap action",
    "Configure the clusters to use the AWS Glue Data Catalog as their Hive metastore"],
 a:[3],
 x:["A cluster that never terminates keeps its local metastore, but the company then pays for an idle cluster all day, and the definitions still live only on one node.",
    "An external metastore in RDS does persist, but it is a database to run and secure, and Athena needs a connector with a Lambda function to query through a Hive metastore.",
    "Export and import scripts are custom work to maintain, and the definitions would still be unavailable to Athena during the day.",
    "AWS recommends the Glue Data Catalog as the metastore when it must persist or be shared by clusters and services; it is managed, and Athena, Amazon EMR and Redshift Spectrum all read the same table definitions from it."],
 ref:[], doc:"glue"},

{id:"q14-010", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"An IoT company writes sensor readings to Amazon S3 under prefixes of the form <code>region/date/hour</code>, and an Amazon Athena table over the data now has several million partitions. Queries that filter on one region and one day still spend most of their run time in planning, and a new hour's data cannot be queried until a scheduled job has registered the new partition. The partition scheme is fixed and known in advance. Which solution addresses both problems with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Run an AWS Glue crawler every hour to add the new partitions to the table",
    "Configure partition projection in the properties of the Athena table",
    "Run <code>MSCK REPAIR TABLE</code> every hour from a scheduled Athena query",
    "Convert the readings to Apache Parquet with a nightly AWS Glue ETL job"],
 a:[1],
 x:["An hourly crawler is still a job to run and wait for, and Athena still has to fetch the metadata of millions of partitions from the Data Catalog when it plans a query.",
    "With partition projection, Athena calculates partition values and locations from table properties instead of looking them up in the Data Catalog, which speeds up planning on highly partitioned tables and removes the need to register new partitions.",
    "<code>MSCK REPAIR TABLE</code> works only with Hive-style partitions (<code>key=value</code> in the path), which these prefixes are not, and it would remain an hourly job.",
    "Parquet reduces the data read from each file; it changes neither the partition metadata lookup nor the need to register each new hour."],
 ref:[], doc:"athena"},

{id:"q14-011", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A company's analysts use Amazon Athena to query a table over five years of clickstream data that is stored in S3 as gzip-compressed CSV files. Nearly every query selects a few of the 80 columns and filters on one year. The analysts know SQL well but have no Apache Spark experience, and the company wants a one-time conversion of the existing data without deploying a new service or cluster. Which solution reduces the data scanned by each query with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Launch an Amazon EMR cluster and run a Spark job that rewrites the files as Parquet",
    "Load the files into an Amazon Redshift cluster and unload the table to S3 as Parquet",
    "Run an Athena CREATE TABLE AS SELECT query that writes Parquet partitioned by year",
    "Split the gzip files into many smaller CSV objects so that Athena reads them in parallel"],
 a:[2],
 x:["This produces Parquet, but it needs a cluster and a Spark job from a team that has neither.",
    "A Redshift cluster would have to be created, loaded and unloaded only to change a file format.",
    "A CTAS query is plain SQL in the service the analysts already use: it writes the query result to S3 in a columnar format such as Parquet, partitioned as specified, so later queries read only the needed columns and the year they filter on.",
    "Smaller CSV files are still read in full, every column of every year, so the amount of data scanned does not go down."],
 ref:[], doc:"athena"},

{id:"q14-012", day:14, dom:1, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A company's data lake has more than 2,000 tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and new tables are added every week. Access is governed with AWS Lake Formation. The governance team grants permissions table by table to dozens of analyst roles and can no longer keep up. Every table is already classified by business domain and sensitivity level, and a role's access should follow that classification without a new grant for each table. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST ongoing administration?",
 o:["Assign LF-Tags for domain and sensitivity to the tables and grant permissions on LF-Tag expressions",
    "Add AWS resource tags to the Glue tables and reference those tags in the Lake Formation grants",
    "Create one Lake Formation data filter for each combination of table and analyst role",
    "Write an IAM policy for each analyst role that lists the S3 prefixes of its tables"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Tag-based access control grants permissions on LF-Tag values instead of on named resources, so a newly classified table is covered by the existing grants. It is the method AWS recommends when there are many Data Catalog objects.",
    "IAM resource tags and LF-Tags are not interchangeable: Lake Formation permissions are granted on LF-Tags, and IAM tags are used in IAM policies.",
    "Data filters restrict columns, rows and cells within a table; creating them per table and per role is the same table-by-table work that does not scale.",
    "Per-role policies that list prefixes must be edited for every new table, which is the administration the team wants to get rid of."],
 ref:[], doc:"lakeformation"},

{id:"q14-013", day:14, dom:1, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A company keeps its S3 data lake and its AWS Glue Data Catalog in a central account, governed with AWS Lake Formation. The fraud team works in a separate AWS account in the same organization and needs to query two of the lake's tables with Amazon Athena from its own account. The team must not see three columns that hold card data, and the company wants neither to copy the data nor to maintain a bucket policy for each consuming account. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Replicate the S3 prefixes of the two tables to a bucket in the fraud team's account",
    "Add a bucket policy that lets the fraud account's Athena role read the tables' prefixes",
    "Create a role in the central account with SELECT on the whole database for the team to assume",
    "Grant Lake Formation permissions on the two tables, excluding the three columns, to the fraud account"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Replication copies the data, which the company wants to avoid, and the copied files still contain the card columns.",
    "A bucket policy gives access to whole objects, card columns included, and is the per-consumer policy the company does not want to maintain.",
    "SELECT on the whole database exposes every table and every column, including the card data.",
    "Lake Formation can grant a table to another account with column filtering; it shares the resource through AWS RAM, and within the same organization the share is available immediately. The data stays where it is."],
 ref:[], doc:"lakeformation"},

{id:"q14-014", day:14, dom:4, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A research company runs Apache Hive and Apache Spark jobs a few times a week, at unpredictable times, on an Amazon EMR cluster that it keeps running on EC2 instances. The cluster is idle for most of the week, and jobs sometimes fail because the cluster is too small for a large input. The company wants to keep its Hive and Spark code unchanged, stop paying for idle instances, and stop sizing clusters. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Convert all of the jobs to AWS Glue ETL jobs with triggers",
    "Run the jobs on Amazon EMR Serverless applications",
    "Move the cluster's task nodes to Spot Instances",
    "Rewrite the jobs as scheduled Amazon Athena SQL queries"],
 a:[1],
 x:["AWS Glue jobs run Spark or Python shell scripts, so the Spark jobs might move, but the Hive jobs would have to be rewritten.",
    "EMR Serverless runs open-source frameworks such as Apache Spark and Apache Hive without a cluster to configure: it works out the resources a job needs, obtains them, and releases them when the job finishes.",
    "Spot task nodes lower the price of part of the cluster, but the primary and core nodes still run all week and the cluster still has to be sized.",
    "Athena could run some of the SQL, but the Hive and Spark code would have to be rewritten rather than kept."],
 ref:[], doc:"emr"},

{id:"q14-015", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics","Streaming"],
 q:"A SaaS company's operations team wants to search application logs by free text, filter them interactively, and watch dashboards of error rates within about a minute of the events. Applications on hundreds of EC2 instances can send their logs to an Amazon Data Firehose stream. No log record may be lost if indexing fails, and the team does not want to run ingestion servers or search software itself. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Deliver the logs to Amazon S3, query them with Amazon Athena, and build Amazon Quick Sight dashboards",
    "Run Logstash and a self-managed OpenSearch cluster on EC2 instances behind a load balancer",
    "Deliver the logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain with S3 backup, and use OpenSearch Dashboards",
    "Deliver the logs to Amazon Redshift through the Firehose stream and query them with SQL"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Athena runs SQL over files in S3, which suits occasional analysis; interactive free-text log search with live dashboards is the use case OpenSearch Service is built for.",
    "This gives the search experience but leaves ingestion servers and the search cluster's software for the team to run.",
    "OpenSearch Service is made for interactive log analytics and real-time application monitoring and includes OpenSearch Dashboards. Firehose loads the domain without servers, with a buffer interval of 0 to 900 seconds, and can back up all records or the failed ones to S3.",
    "Redshift is a data warehouse for SQL analytics on structured data; it is not a free-text search engine with log dashboards."],
 ref:[], doc:"opensearch"},

{id:"q14-016", day:14, dom:2, tags:["Analytics","High availability design"],
 q:"A company runs a production Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for product search. The domain has two data nodes in one Availability Zone, no dedicated master nodes, and indexes without replicas. A recent Availability Zone disruption made search unavailable for hours. The company wants the domain to keep serving search and indexing requests if an Availability Zone fails, without a drop in performance during the failure. Which configuration should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["One Availability Zone with six larger data nodes and hourly automated snapshots of the domain",
    "One Availability Zone with three dedicated master nodes added to the two existing data nodes",
    "Two Availability Zones with one data node in each, one replica per index, and no dedicated master nodes",
    "Multi-AZ with Standby, with three dedicated master nodes and two replicas for each index"],
 a:[3],
 x:["More nodes and snapshots in the same zone do not keep the domain serving when that zone fails; snapshots only allow a restore afterwards.",
    "Dedicated master nodes make the cluster more stable, but every node is still in the one zone that can fail.",
    "Multi-AZ without Standby survives a zone failure only with less capacity left, AWS no longer recommends it for new production domains, and there are no dedicated master nodes for cluster stability.",
    "Multi-AZ with Standby is what AWS recommends for production: each of three zones holds a full copy of the data and one zone's nodes stand by, so after a failure there is no redistribution of data and performance is unaffected."],
 ref:[], doc:"opensearch"},

{id:"q14-017", day:14, dom:1, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A retail chain publishes one sales dashboard in Amazon Quick Sight to 400 store managers. The dashboard reads a single dataset that contains the sales of every store. Each manager must be able to see only the rows of their own store, regional directors must see all stores of their region, and the BI team does not want to maintain a separate dataset or dashboard for each store. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add row-level security to the dataset with a permissions dataset that maps users and groups to stores",
    "Create a separate SPICE dataset for each store and share each one with that store's manager",
    "Add a store parameter to the dashboard and instruct each manager to select their own store",
    "Create a copy of the dashboard for each store with a filter that is fixed to that store"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Row-level security restricts what readers see in a dataset through rules that name users or groups and the field values they may see, so one dataset and one dashboard serve every manager and director. Quick Sight is a feature of Amazon Quick (formerly Amazon QuickSight), the name the exam guide lists.",
    "This isolates the data but creates 400 datasets to load and maintain, which the BI team wants to avoid.",
    "A parameter is a choice the viewer makes; nothing would stop a manager from selecting another store.",
    "This means 400 dashboards to maintain, and the regional directors would still need another view."],
 ref:[], doc:"quick"},

{id:"q14-018", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"An insurance company wants to enrich its claims models with commercial weather and property data from several third-party providers. Today each provider delivers differently, through FTP credentials, emailed links, or its own API, each with a separate contract and invoice. The company wants one place to find, subscribe to, and pay for such data sets, and wants new revisions loaded into its S3 data lake with little custom integration. Which AWS service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon AppFlow","AWS Transfer Family","AWS Data Exchange","AWS DataSync"],
 a:[2],
 x:["AppFlow moves data between SaaS applications such as Salesforce and AWS services; it is not a catalog of third-party data products with subscriptions.",
    "Transfer Family gives managed SFTP, FTPS and FTP endpoints in front of S3; every provider would still deliver, contract and bill in its own way.",
    "Data Exchange is the service for finding, subscribing to and using third-party data: subscriptions are managed in one place, data can be loaded directly into S3 through its API, and an event is sent each time a provider publishes a new revision.",
    "DataSync transfers files and objects between storage systems; it has no catalog, subscriptions or billing for third-party data."],
 ref:[], doc:"dataexchange"},

{id:"q14-019", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A sales organization uses Salesforce. Whenever a sales opportunity is created or changed there, the record must be copied into a table in Amazon Redshift for near-real-time reporting. A custom field that holds the customer's phone number must be masked before the record is stored, and records without an account ID must be left out. The team wants no code to write and no servers or connectors to maintain. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Write a Lambda function that polls the Salesforce API every minute, masks the field, and inserts rows into Redshift",
    "Create an event-triggered Amazon AppFlow flow to Amazon Redshift with a masking transformation and a validation",
    "Export the opportunities from Salesforce as CSV files every night and load them with the Redshift COPY command",
    "Create an Amazon Data Firehose stream with a Lambda transformation function and a Redshift destination"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Polling code, masking code and inserts are all custom code to write and operate.",
    "AppFlow transfers data between SaaS applications and AWS services such as Redshift without code. A flow can run on an event in the source application, such as the creation of an opportunity, and can mask fields and validate records as part of the flow.",
    "A nightly export arrives up to a day late and still needs someone or something to produce and load the files.",
    "Firehose can load Redshift, but it takes records from producers that put them into it; something would still have to read the changes from Salesforce, and the masking would be a function to write."],
 ref:[], doc:"appflow"},

// ---- Redshift ----

{id:"q14-020", day:14, dom:2, tags:["Redshift","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A company runs an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster in eu-west-1 that is encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key. A new policy requires that the data warehouse can be restored in eu-central-1 if eu-west-1 becomes unavailable, with at most 24 hours of data loss. The company does not want to pay for a second running cluster. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Convert the cluster to a Multi-AZ deployment that runs in two Availability Zones",
    "Enable automatic cross-Region snapshot copy from eu-west-1 to eu-central-1 for the cluster",
    "Turn on concurrency scaling so that additional cluster capacity is added automatically",
    "Schedule a nightly UNLOAD of every table to an S3 bucket in the eu-west-1 Region",
    "Create a snapshot copy grant in eu-central-1 so that Amazon Redshift can use a KMS key there"],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["A Multi-AZ deployment keeps the warehouse running through an Availability Zone failure; both zones are in eu-west-1.",
    "Redshift can copy a cluster's automated and manual snapshots to one other Region as they are created, and a new cluster can be restored from them there. Automated snapshots are taken about every eight hours or sooner, and no second cluster runs in the meantime.",
    "Concurrency scaling adds capacity for bursts of queries; it does not place any data in another Region.",
    "The unloaded files stay in eu-west-1, so they are as unavailable as the cluster when that Region fails.",
    "Snapshots of a KMS-encrypted cluster can be copied to another Region only after a grant lets Redshift use a customer managed key in the destination Region; that grant is selected when the copy is enabled."],
 ref:[], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"q14-021", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Redshift","Analytics"],
 q:"A telecom company stores seven years of call detail records as Parquet files in S3, partitioned by month. Dashboards on an Amazon Redshift RA3 cluster query the most recent three months all day long and must return in seconds. Older data is needed only for occasional audits, which sometimes join it with the recent data in a single SQL statement. The company wants the best dashboard performance without paying to keep all seven years in the cluster. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Load the most recent three months into Redshift tables with the COPY command",
    "Load all seven years into Redshift tables and add nodes as the data grows",
    "Define an external schema and query the older months in S3 with Redshift Spectrum",
    "Query all seven years through Redshift Spectrum and keep no data in the cluster",
    "Move the older months to S3 Glacier Deep Archive and restore them before each audit"],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Data that dashboards query all day belongs in the warehouse itself: Redshift's guidance is to ingest data into the warehouse for high-performance analytics, and COPY loads it from S3.",
    "This would perform well, but the company would pay to hold seven years in the cluster for data that is used a few times a year.",
    "Redshift Spectrum queries files in S3 through external tables without loading them, and one SQL statement can join an external table with a local table, which covers the audits.",
    "Every dashboard query would then read files in S3 all day; nothing would use the warehouse's own storage, which is where Redshift delivers its best query performance.",
    "Archived objects cannot be queried or joined until they have been restored, which takes hours, and the audits need a single SQL statement."],
 ref:[], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"q14-022", day:14, dom:4, tags:["Redshift"],
 q:"A company's Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster is sized correctly for its normal workload. For a few hours at each month end, hundreds of finance users run reports at the same time and queries wait in the queue for minutes. The bursts start at unpredictable times, the cluster must stay available for reads and writes throughout, and the company does not want to pay for a larger cluster during the whole month. Which solution is the MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Run an elastic resize that adds nodes before each month end and removes them afterward",
    "Double the number of nodes in the cluster and keep the larger cluster permanently",
    "Restore the latest snapshot to a second cluster at each month end for the finance users",
    "Turn on concurrency scaling for the queue that the finance reports run in"],
 a:[3],
 x:["An elastic resize must be timed by someone although the bursts are unpredictable, and the cluster is unavailable for several minutes while it resizes.",
    "This removes the queueing but pays for twice the capacity during the weeks when it is not needed.",
    "A restored cluster holds the data as of the snapshot, has to be created and deleted by hand each month, and the users would have to be pointed at it.",
    "With concurrency scaling, Redshift adds cluster capacity automatically when queries would otherwise queue, the main cluster stays fully available, and the extra clusters are charged only for the time they are running queries."],
 ref:[], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"q14-023", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Redshift","Analytics"],
 q:"A company wants a SQL analytics platform with no clusters or nodes to size. Finance analysts will run enterprise reports all day that join a dozen large, structured tables and use many subqueries, and the reports must return quickly and consistently. The data will be loaded from the company's operational databases. A solutions architect is comparing the options. Which service is the BEST fit?",
 o:["Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift Serverless","Amazon EMR Serverless","Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with Aurora Replicas"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Athena is also serverless, but it is aimed at interactive queries and exploration of data in S3 and other sources; AWS points to Redshift for enterprise reporting with very complex joins and subqueries.",
    "Redshift, serverless or provisioned, is the data warehouse AWS recommends for enterprise reporting and BI with complex SQL over large structured data, and the serverless option provisions and scales the capacity automatically.",
    "EMR Serverless runs Spark and Hive jobs written in code; it is a data processing runtime, not a warehouse for BI reports.",
    "Aurora is a transactional relational database whose instances have to be chosen and sized; large analytic joins are the job of a data warehouse."],
 ref:["d10c3"], doc:"redshift"},

// ---- AI/ML services ----

{id:"q14-024", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"A company receives thousands of product reviews and support emails each day as plain text. It wants to know automatically whether each message is positive, negative, neutral or mixed, which products and people it mentions, and whether it contains personal data such as phone numbers. The company has no training data and no data scientists. Which service meets these requirements with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Amazon Textract","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Lex"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Textract gets text, forms and tables out of scanned documents; the messages are already text, and it does not judge sentiment.",
    "Transcribe converts audio to text; there is no audio here.",
    "Comprehend analyses text with pre-trained models and returns sentiment (positive, neutral, negative or mixed), entities such as people and items, and personally identifiable information, with no training data needed.",
    "Lex builds conversational bots that talk with users; it does not analyse a collection of messages."],
 ref:[], doc:"comprehend"},

{id:"q14-025", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"An airline wants customers to change seat assignments by typing or speaking in its mobile app. The solution must work out what the customer wants, ask follow-up questions for missing details such as the flight number, and then call a Lambda function that makes the change. Which service should the airline use?",
 o:["Amazon Lex","Amazon Polly","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Comprehend"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Lex builds conversational interfaces for voice and text: it understands the intent behind the input, manages the dialog, and integrates with Lambda to carry out the request.",
    "Polly turns text into speech; it could speak a reply but does not understand requests or manage a conversation.",
    "Transcribe turns speech into text; working out the intent and running the dialog would still have to be built.",
    "Comprehend extracts insights such as entities and sentiment from documents; it does not conduct a conversation."],
 ref:[], doc:"lex"},

{id:"q14-026", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"An e-learning company wants every article in its catalog to be available as natural-sounding audio so that visually impaired students can listen to it. The articles already exist as text in each language the company supports, and they change too often for recordings by voice actors. Which service meets this need with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Translate","Amazon Lex","Amazon Polly"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Transcribe works in the opposite direction: it converts audio into text.",
    "Translate converts text from one language into another; the result is still text, and the articles already exist in each language.",
    "Lex is for conversational bots; reading an article aloud needs no dialog.",
    "Polly converts text into lifelike speech in multiple languages and voices, and accessibility applications for visually impaired people are one of its common use cases."],
 ref:[], doc:"polly"},

{id:"q14-027", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"A car-sharing company wants to verify new members by comparing a selfie from its mobile app with the photo on the uploaded driver's license. It also wants to label the vehicle photos in its S3 bucket with the objects and scenes they show, so that they can be searched. The company has no machine learning expertise. Which service should it use?",
 o:["Amazon Textract","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon SageMaker AI"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Textract extracts text and data from documents, for example the fields of the license; it does not compare faces or label the content of photos.",
    "Rekognition is the image and video analysis service: its APIs detect objects and scenes and detect, analyse and compare faces, for use cases such as user verification, and it needs no machine learning expertise.",
    "Comprehend analyses text, not images.",
    "A custom vision model could be built and trained in SageMaker AI, but that takes the expertise the company lacks when a ready-made API exists."],
 ref:[], doc:"rekognition"},

{id:"q14-028", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"A fintech company's data scientists have written their own fraud-scoring algorithm in an open-source framework. They need to train it on several terabytes of proprietary transaction data and host the trained model behind an endpoint for real-time predictions. They want managed infrastructure for both steps instead of EC2 fleets that they patch themselves. Which service should they use?",
 o:["Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Textract"],
 a:[0],
 x:["SageMaker AI is the fully managed service for building, training and deploying machine learning models into a hosted environment, with support for bringing your own algorithms and frameworks.",
    "Comprehend offers natural language processing on text with pre-trained or custom text models; it does not train and host an arbitrary algorithm on transaction data.",
    "Rekognition analyses images and video; it is not a platform for the team's own model.",
    "Textract extracts text and data from documents; it does not train or host models written by the customer."],
 ref:[], doc:"sagemaker"},

{id:"q14-029", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"A mortgage lender scans thousands of paper application forms each day into PDF files in S3. It needs the applicant's name and income, and the rows of a table of liabilities, extracted as structured data. Many forms contain handwritten entries. Which service meets these requirements with the LEAST development effort?",
 o:["Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Textract","Amazon Transcribe"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Rekognition can detect text in images, but turning a form's fields and a table's rows into structured data is what Textract's document analysis does.",
    "Comprehend analyses text that has already been extracted; it does not read scanned forms and tables.",
    "Textract detects typed and handwritten text in documents and extracts text, forms and tables from documents with structured data.",
    "Transcribe converts speech in audio into text; scanned paper contains no audio."],
 ref:[], doc:"textract"},

{id:"q14-030", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"A contact center records every customer call as an audio file in S3. The quality team wants a searchable text version of each call that separates the agent's speech from the customer's, and wants the product names, places and key phrases mentioned in each call picked out automatically. The company does not want to train models. Which combination of services meets these requirements with the LEAST effort? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Amazon Polly","Amazon Lex","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Textract","Amazon Comprehend"],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Polly produces speech from text, the opposite of what is needed.",
    "Lex runs live conversations with a bot; it does not process recorded calls.",
    "Transcribe converts media files in S3 into text as batch jobs and can partition the speech of individual speakers.",
    "Textract reads scanned documents, not audio recordings.",
    "Comprehend takes the transcript text and returns entities such as items and places, and key phrases, with its pre-trained models."],
 ref:[], doc:"transcribe"},

{id:"q14-031", day:14, dom:3, tags:["AI/ML services"],
 q:"An online marketplace wants buyers and sellers who write in different languages to chat with each other. Each chat message must be shown to the recipient in the recipient's own language moments after it is sent, and the company does not want to manage translation models. Which service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Polly","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Translate"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Comprehend can identify the dominant language of a message and its sentiment, but it does not convert the text into another language.",
    "Polly speaks text aloud; the chat needs written text in another language.",
    "Transcribe converts speech into text; the messages are already text.",
    "Translate is the managed text translation service that works on demand, and translating interpersonal communication such as chat is one of its documented use cases."],
 ref:[], doc:"translate"},

// ---- Purpose-built databases ----

{id:"q14-032", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases"],
 q:"A content platform migrated its MongoDB workload to an Amazon DocumentDB instance-based cluster that has one primary instance and no replicas. Reporting queries now compete with the application's writes, and the primary's CPU stays above 90%. The reports can tolerate data that is a fraction of a second old. The team wants the reports to stop competing with the writes while it keeps its MongoDB drivers and its data model. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Create a second DocumentDB cluster and copy the data to it each night with <code>mongodump</code>",
    "Add replica instances to the cluster and read from them with the <code>secondaryPreferred</code> read preference",
    "Put a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster between the application and the DocumentDB cluster",
    "Convert the collections to Amazon DynamoDB tables and add global secondary indexes for the reports"],
 a:[1],
 x:["A nightly copy makes the reports up to a day old instead of a fraction of a second, and adds a second cluster and a copy job to run.",
    "A DocumentDB cluster can have up to 15 replicas that serve reads from the same cluster volume, usually less than 100 milliseconds behind the primary; connecting as a replica set with <code>secondaryPreferred</code> sends reads to them and frees the primary for writes.",
    "DAX is a cache that is API-compatible with DynamoDB; it does not sit in front of a MongoDB-compatible database.",
    "This changes both the data model and the drivers, which the team wants to keep."],
 ref:[], doc:"documentdb"},

{id:"q14-033", day:14, dom:2, tags:["Purpose-built databases"],
 q:"A gaming company stores player profiles in Apache Cassandra clusters that it operates on EC2 instances in three Regions. Applications read and write the profiles through CQL drivers in the Region nearest to each player. The company wants to stop operating Cassandra nodes and cross-Region replication, keep its CQL code, and keep serving reads and writes locally in the other Regions if one Region is degraded. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon Keyspaces with a multi-Region keyspace that is replicated to the three Regions",
    "Amazon DynamoDB global tables with a replica table in each of the three Regions",
    "Amazon Keyspaces tables in a single Region that all three Regions connect to",
    "Apache Cassandra on EC2 Auto Scaling groups with EBS snapshots copied across Regions"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Keyspaces is serverless and Cassandra-compatible, so the CQL code stays, and its multi-Region replication is fully managed and active-active: every Region serves reads and writes on its own, which also covers a degraded Region.",
    "Global tables also give multi-Region, multi-active replication, but the application would have to be rewritten from CQL to the DynamoDB API.",
    "This removes the nodes to operate, but two Regions lose local reads and writes, and a problem in the single Region affects all players.",
    "The company would still install, patch and scale Cassandra itself, and snapshots are backups, not replication that serves local writes."],
 ref:[], doc:"keyspaces"},

{id:"q14-034", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases"],
 q:"A cybersecurity company models hosts, users and network connections as a property graph in an open-source graph database that it runs on EC2 instances. Its application queries the graph with Apache TinkerPop Gremlin traversals. The company wants a fully managed service with read replicas and replication across Availability Zones, and it wants to keep its Gremlin queries. Which service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility)","Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra)","Amazon Neptune","Amazon MemoryDB"],
 a:[2],
 x:["DocumentDB is compatible with the MongoDB API and stores documents; it does not run Gremlin traversals.",
    "Keyspaces is compatible with Apache Cassandra and is queried with CQL, not Gremlin.",
    "Neptune is the managed graph database: it supports Apache TinkerPop Gremlin and openCypher for property graphs and SPARQL for RDF, has read replicas and replication across Availability Zones, and an existing TinkerPop application can be pointed at its endpoint.",
    "MemoryDB is compatible with Valkey and Redis OSS data structures and commands, not with graph query languages."],
 ref:[], doc:"neptune"},

{id:"q14-035", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases","Caching"],
 q:"A ride-hailing company's dispatch microservice keeps driver locations and trip state in Redis OSS data structures. Today it writes every change to an Amazon RDS database for durability and to a cache for speed, and the two regularly drift apart. The company wants a single Redis OSS-compatible data store that gives microsecond reads, keeps every acknowledged write if a node or an Availability Zone fails, and makes the separate database unnecessary. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached with nodes in several Availability Zones",
    "Amazon ElastiCache for Redis OSS with asynchronous replicas and daily backups",
    "Amazon DynamoDB with a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster in front of the table",
    "Amazon MemoryDB with a primary node and replicas in several Availability Zones"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Memcached is the simplest model with simple data types; it offers neither the Redis OSS data structures nor a durable store.",
    "With asynchronous replication a write that was acknowledged can be lost when the primary fails over, and a daily backup loses up to a day.",
    "DynamoDB is durable, but it is not Redis OSS-compatible, so the microservice would have to be rewritten.",
    "MemoryDB is a Valkey- and Redis OSS-compatible in-memory database that records writes in a Multi-AZ transactional log, so data is not lost across failovers, and it can replace a database-plus-cache pair. Note that ElastiCache for Valkey 9.0 and later now also offers durability and AWS recommends ElastiCache for new workloads; that choice is not offered here."],
 ref:[], doc:"memorydb"},

{id:"q14-036", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Purpose-built databases"],
 q:"A manufacturer collects temperature and vibration readings from its factory equipment every second. Its engineers already use open-source InfluxDB on premises, with their own collection agents and dashboards, and queries on recent data must return in milliseconds. The company has never used Amazon Timestream and wants a managed time-series database on AWS that lets it keep the InfluxDB APIs and tools. Which service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon Timestream for LiveAnalytics","Amazon Timestream for InfluxDB","Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra)","Amazon DynamoDB with a timestamp sort key"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Timestream for LiveAnalytics no longer accepts new customers, and AWS directs time-series workloads to Timestream for InfluxDB.",
    "Timestream for InfluxDB is the managed time-series engine that runs open-source InfluxDB, so existing InfluxDB code and tools keep working, and it answers queries with single-digit millisecond response times.",
    "Keyspaces is a Cassandra-compatible store queried with CQL; the InfluxDB APIs and tools would not work with it.",
    "Time-series data can be modelled in DynamoDB, but the application and tools would have to be rewritten for the DynamoDB API."],
 ref:[], doc:"timestream"},

// ---- Caching ----

{id:"q14-037", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Caching","DynamoDB"],
 q:"A travel site stores bookings in Amazon DynamoDB. Its home page shows each signed-in user a personalized summary that the application builds from about 40 DynamoDB queries and a call to a partner's pricing API, which takes 2 seconds in total. A summary may be up to 10 minutes old. The team wants repeat views of the page to return in a few milliseconds and wants to reduce the number of calls to the partner API. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster and send the 40 queries through it",
    "Raise the table's maximum read capacity in its DynamoDB auto scaling settings",
    "Store each computed summary in Amazon ElastiCache with a 10-minute TTL and read it first",
    "Create a global secondary index that projects the attributes shown in the summary"],
 a:[2],
 x:["DAX caches the results of DynamoDB reads in its item and query caches, so the 40 queries get faster, but the page would still be assembled and the partner API still called on every view.",
    "More read capacity prevents throttling; it does not shorten the work of 40 queries plus an external call.",
    "ElastiCache is an in-memory key-value store that holds whatever the application puts in it, here the finished summary: repeat views are one cache read, and the queries and the partner call run only when the entry has expired.",
    "An index might replace some of the queries, but the summary is still rebuilt and the partner API still called for every page view."],
 ref:["d3c4"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"q14-038", day:14, dom:3, tags:["Caching"],
 q:"A document-sharing service stores metadata for 300 million files in Amazon Aurora and plans to add Amazon ElastiCache. Metadata is written once, at upload. About 3% of the files are ever opened again, and those are opened many times. A slightly slower first read of a file is acceptable, the cache must stay small, and the application must keep working if a cache node is replaced by an empty one. Which caching strategy fits this access pattern?",
 o:["Lazy loading: read from the cache first and fill it from the database on a miss",
    "Write-through: write each file's metadata to the cache whenever it is written to the database",
    "Preloading: copy all metadata into the cache with a batch job and refresh it every night",
    "Lazy loading with a time to live of one second on every key that is cached"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Lazy loading caches only data that is actually requested, so the cache holds the 3% that is read; a replaced node simply fills up again from misses, at the price of a slower first read, which is acceptable here.",
    "Write-through puts every upload into the cache although most data is never read, which wastes memory, and a new empty node lacks data until it is written again.",
    "Preloading 300 million entries is the opposite of a small cache, and an empty replacement node would have to be reloaded before it helps.",
    "With a one-second expiry nearly every repeat read is a miss that goes to the database, so the cache takes almost no load off Aurora."],
 ref:[], doc:"elasticache"},

// ---- DynamoDB ----

{id:"q14-039", day:14, dom:3, tags:["DynamoDB","Lambda"],
 q:"An online store keeps its orders in an Amazon DynamoDB table. When an order's status changes, a loyalty service must receive both the previous and the new version of the item within seconds, in the sequence in which the changes to that item were made. The application that writes the orders must not be modified, and the team does not want to poll the table. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Schedule a Lambda function every minute that scans the table for changed items",
    "Enable DynamoDB Streams on the table with the new and old images view type",
    "Add a global secondary index to the table with the status as its partition key",
    "Create a Lambda function that has the table's stream as its event source",
    "Enable Time to Live (TTL) on the table with the status attribute as its TTL attribute"],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["A scheduled scan is polling, reads the whole table each time, and cannot return the previous version of an item.",
    "DynamoDB Streams records every item-level change in near-real time, keeps the changes to each item in the order they were made, and can include the item as it was before and after the change. The writing application is untouched.",
    "An index makes it cheaper to query by status, but something would still have to poll it, and it holds no previous versions.",
    "A Lambda function associated with the stream is invoked with the stream records as they appear, so the loyalty service is fed without any polling code.",
    "TTL deletes items after an expiry time; it does not report status changes."],
 ref:[], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q14-040", day:14, dom:2, tags:["DynamoDB","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A payments company keeps account balances in an Amazon DynamoDB table in one Region. Regulators now require that the application continue in another Region if a Region fails, with a recovery point objective of zero: no write that was confirmed to a client may be lost. A strongly consistent read in any of the Regions must always return the latest balance. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["A global table with the default multi-Region eventual consistency and replicas in two Regions",
    "Point-in-time recovery on the table and backups copied to a second Region with AWS Backup",
    "A DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster in a second Region in front of the existing table",
    "A global table configured for multi-Region strong consistency across three Regions"],
 a:[3],
 x:["In the default mode changes are replicated asynchronously, typically within a second, so the latest writes may not have left a Region that fails, and a strongly consistent read can return stale data for an item last updated in another Region.",
    "Backups are restored to a new table, which takes time and brings back the data as of the backup, not every confirmed write.",
    "DAX is a cache that serves eventually consistent reads; it is neither a copy of the table in another Region nor a recovery mechanism.",
    "With multi-Region strong consistency a write is replicated synchronously to at least one other Region before it is confirmed, and strongly consistent reads on any replica return the latest version. Such a table is deployed in exactly three Regions."],
 ref:["d9c4"], doc:"global-tables"}

);
